  "query_preview": "../FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/\n\nAn H5 longwave trof will intensify across the Intermountain West \nearly Friday morning. Lee cyclogenesis occurs with the sfc \nfeature tracking northeast toward the Dakotas. A tight pressure \ngradient should develop over most of the Plains, and combined \nwith afternoon mixing into an H8 40 to 50 kt, expect gusty south\nsoutheasterly sfc winds of 40 to 45 mph. Another warm day will \nbe on store with highs reaching the upper 70s to low 80s. Given \nthe strong winds and dry conditions, some fire weather concerns \nmay arise, particularly in areas where crops have managed to dry\nout and become cured. Minimum RH currently ranges from 30 to \n40% for most of the area that day, but could see more of a drop \ndepending on how deep we can mix. At this time, portions of \nnortheast Nebraska have at least some risk for meeting Red Flag \nWarning criteria, however, the lowest RH values of 25 to 30%\nstill appear concentrated along the Nebraska/Kansas border area\nand away from the highest winds.\n\nWhile not overly strong, should see some forcing for ascent \nmove in by late Friday into Saturday. This should work with \nsome low level moisture to generate scattered showers and \nperhaps some storms. PoPs of 30 to 60% are expected for \nprimarily northeast Nebraska on Saturday. As the H5 wave pivots \nto the northeast, another disturbance will pivot from the \nsouthwest and eject toward the Plains on Sunday, leading to \ncontinued shower and storm chances, this time areawide at 60 to\n80%. Chances largely decrease by Tuesday as the wave departs \nthe region. By Monday night, most locations should see anywhere \nfrom a quarter to half inch of rain, while far northeast \nNebraska could see totals of an inch or more. Temperatures will\nbe on a downward trend from Saturday onward, reaching the 50s \nand 60s by Tuesday.",
